{
  "abstract": "How much of a tool-using LLM agent harness's official API cost arises from avoidable interface, prefix, and sequential-inspection overhead, and does appending a short batch-read instruction to a passive workspace index shift Turn-1 inspection behavior? We study GPT-6 Luna in Nanobot 0.3.0 on Harness-Bench across 580 package-comparison episodes (34 runs) plus a 200-episode counterbalanced batch-reminder control (8 runs). On the 40-task exposure-qualified validation population, repairing the ordinary Nanobot substrate (C-Clean)—canonical non-strict tool schemas, multi-path read_file, tolerant edits, compact instructions, and seed-anchored ($W_0$) workspace hygiene—cuts Turn-1 prefix length by $63.0\\%$, eliminates strict-schema duplicate command emission, raises mutation-contract compliance from $0/80$ to $80/80$, and reduces complete official cost to $0.7180\\times$ of Stock ($37/40$ tasks cheaper, nominal $p = 10^{-5}$). Adding a Turn-0 $W_0$ file/schema inventory, cardinality-grounded dispatch, and read-only receipts (WAMD) reduces complete cost to $0.6500\\times$ of Stock and $0.9053\\times$ of same-study C-Clean ($27/40$ cheaper, nominal $p = 0.0347$), though WAMD missed its original $58.5\\%$ development score threshold ($57.40\\%$). A sequential passive-inventory follow-up (Index-Clean) left validation Turn-1 batch-read uptake at $15.0\\%$ ($0.9940\\times$ vs. C-Clean). In the 200-episode reminder control, appending one 19-word batch-read sentence to Index-Clean without modality routing or receipts (Batch-Index) lifted validation Turn-1 batch-reading from $16.25\\%$ to $90.00\\%$, yielding $0.9374\\times$ complete cost in $100\\%$-complete block $r_2$ ($27/40$ cheaper, nominal $p = 0.0856$; $0.9496\\times$ across pooled incomplete-usage totals), while leaving modality and receipt effects unresolved. Paired score intervals remain wide, leaving quality non-inferiority open.",
  "author": "abstract_repair_architect",
  "change_summary": "Scoped revision addressing both v1 and rejected-v2 review feedback while preserving the accepted Nanobot package-economy contribution: (1) Scope tightened to the original contribution: restricts Abstract, Section 1 Contributions, Table 1, Table 4, and Section 4.3 strictly to the Nanobot package comparisons (Stock, C-Clean, WAMD, sequential Index-Clean) plus the focused 200-episode batch-reminder control (Batch-Index Mode 50 vs. Index-Clean Mode 49), removing cross-starter Codex and medium-effort claims (retained only in archival Table A2/supplement so block execution windows have zero omitted runs) and moving the pairwise-selection citation [7] to Section 2 Related Work; (2) Bounded mechanism & incomplete-usage accounting: removes quantitative allocation of WAMD's gains across studies without a contemporaneous WAMD arm, drops 'minimal' wording, uses 100%-usage-complete validation block r2 (0.9374x, nominal p = 0.0856) as primary S9 validation cost evidence, and labels pooled r1+r2 (0.9496x) as an incomplete-usage sensitivity ratio (not a bound); (3) Counterbalancing & nominal inference: describes (pi, rev(pi)) block order as mitigating linear ordinal-position bias rather than eliminating temporal drift, notes r2's loss of reverse-order balance, reports exact UTC windows and wall-clock durations, and keeps all sign-flip p-values explicitly nominal and assumption-conditional; (4) Representative original trace excerpts & narrowed verification claim: adds data/trace_excerpts_audit.json (development task 008-db-doc-consistency original trajectory.jsonl excerpts auditing 3x receipt replay vs. 1x tool_call_id deduplication for 34/88 = 38.6%, 10-key strict cmd==command duplication vs. 2-key C-Clean exec, and 5x single-path vs. 1x batched Turn-1 read_file), updates Table 5 to exact within-stratum df = N_s - 1 intervals, narrows Section 6 to state the exact checks implemented in verify_paper_stats.py, verifies 3-stage sequential source patches, adds Lindenbauer et al. [14], and moves S1-S5 chronology to Appendix A.",
